>
  <h2>What’s an error page?</h2>
  <p>It’s a screen like “Page not found” or “Down for maintenance.” Lots of people see one every day, and then they leave.</p>

  <h2>What does doorbell.life put on it?</h2>
  <p>People and animals nearby who need help: missing people, shelter animals waiting for a home, and more. A regular error page shows one notice. A maintenance page, where visitors have to wait, rotates through several. Each one includes a phone number for the shelter or tip line.</p>
  <p>In the US today, that means FBI missing-person posters, adoptable animals from the Montgomery County, Maryland shelter, and FBI statistics on missing persons. Other US shelters, lost pets, and blood donation aren’t covered yet.</p>

  <h2>Who can install it? What does it cost?</h2>
  <p>Any website can install it for free, with no account. A site owner pastes one line of code or installs the WordPress plugin, and that’s it. See <a href="/install/usa/">Install</a>.</p>

  <h2>Does it affect the website or its visitors?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The error page is still an error page. A 404 stays a 404 and never sends you somewhere else.</li>
    <li>If the data can’t load, nothing is shown, and visitors see the site’s original page.</li>
    <li>No cookies, nothing stored in the browser, no IP addresses kept.</li>
    <li>No ads on error pages.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs it?</h2>
  <p>For now, a single maintainer runs it as an individual. All the code is public on GitHub for anyone to review. A formal organization will be set up later.</p>
</AboutPage>
